  "full_response": "The publish-subscribe (pub-sub) messaging pattern is an implementation of asynchronous service-to-service message-oriented middleware, based on observing topics. It is designed for providing a flexible and interoperable means for message exchange between distributed components of an application.\n\nIn this pattern, publishers (publishers) send messages (publishes) that are received by subscribers (subscribers) that have joined the interests (subscribed to) a particular topic. A topic is a kind of channel or feed that carriers messages, filtering them so only interested subscribers receive them. Subscribers can optionally filter messages using a interest filter; only if the filter is satisfied, the message appears. They may publish their own messages as well; or in reality, the last named subscribers publish messages difference analysis about perceived inconsistencies (who is causing a divergence) and how to correct it; that is, subscribers are both receivers and contributors. However, a normal subscriber will receive only topic routes it is interested in, and as a result, effectively receive only their own data.\n\nPub-sub offers the following design benefits:\n- Loose coupling: Publishers and subscribers need not be directly connected to each other.\n- Message way for decoupling: Messages are transmitted over various paths; thereby providing long-lived, routed messages independent of users and processes.\n- Suited for asynchronous communication where publishers and subscribers do not expect a timely message delivery.\n\n**Examples of pub-sub pattern:**\n\n1. News Aggregator: Here, news publishers send their articles to a news feed service. Various news portal services (publishers) can freely publish their articles to the news feed. The public can subscribe (subscribers) to their preferred news portal, and get updates on their portal in real-time whenever a new article is published.\n\n2. Social Platform: In the case of a social media platform, users (publishers) can post statuses (publishes) and other content of interest to their followers (subscribers). Social media companies will then display the latest published content stand-alone or grouped by topic (topics) through their mobile app for each subscribed follower.\n\n3. Stock Market Trading: In the stock market, events such as a stock's closing price are published to a trading venue which then disseminates this information to its subscribers who can be traders or investors subscribing anonymously to the stream of the trading venue. The accurate and continuous, real-time updates provided to investors by the trading venue enable informed trading decisions.\n\nIn a nutshell, the pub-sub messaging pattern makes it possible to send information wherever it's needed and be able to easily alter the",
